    {
      "sectionCode": "book6-article-018-body",
      "heading": "Reading the Book VI structure",
      "anchor": "article-body",
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "Cross-border finance, multinational production and computerized information processing jointly increased the coordination speed of the global system. Financial networks accelerated capital mobility, firms distributed production across countries, and computers reduced the cost of processing information in complex organizations."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "This does not mean state borders disappear. Firms and capital often organize around differences in law, taxation, talent and markets, while digital capacity still depends on energy, infrastructure and institutions."
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph",
          "text": "This article remains within the completed Book VI manuscript; B6-WEB-B does not add live current-data population. Subjective experience and structural conditions remain distinct, and no single measure is used to infer a person or an entire society."
        }
      ]
    }
